Falsifies `system_tests::user::tenth_pass_map_gate_tests::one_physical_page_is_never_writable_here_and_executable_there` (claim 11).

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is in the tree: `AddressSpace::MAP_INTO` never checks whether the frame it maps is
already mapped in the same space. `MAP_RW` at one `va` and `MAP_CODE` at another leave every
page-table leaf honest while the physical page is writable and executable through the two
aliases. A builder holding `WRITE` on a space and a `READ | WRITE` frame reaches it with ordinary
capabilities and no defect in the grant chain. The per-leaf proofs the row cites survive; the
claim as published does not. The pass is milestone 936 (a tenth outsider pass attacks the
confinement claim); on 2026-10-27 (UTC) the architect ruled that the escape sits on a shipped path
and that its fix is mandatory.

So the patch introduces no defect. It turns the witness's opt-in on, which is what
`NIFE_ESCAPE_WITNESS=1` does by hand, and the replay boots the escape as it stands. The fix
milestone re-cuts this patch to undo its fix instead, and drops the opt-in.

Expected to fail: the witness assert, reporting `one physical page is writable at <writable va>
and executable at <executable va>: every leaf is honest and the page is both, which is W^X defeated
by alias (claim 11)`.

Replayed on 2026-10-27 (UTC) against this tree, one ISA at a time under QEMU 11.1.2 with
`cargo xtask test --arch <isa> --test tenth_pass_map_gate`: red at exactly that assertion on all
three, with the two boundary tests in the module green beside it. aarch64 reported writable at
0xffffffffe000 and executable at 0xfffffffff000; riscv64, 0x3fffffe000 and 0x3ffffff000; x86_64,
0x7fffffffe000 and 0x7ffffffff000.

